        { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Where Langer would agree with Collingwood that art involves a transformation of raw feeling into something articulate and shareable, she would resist his claim that this process is essentially about the artist\'s private emotional clarification. For Langer, the artist\'s individual emotion is almost beside the point; what matters is the objective symbolic form that the work creates, which refers to the general structure of feeling rather than any particular person\'s state of mind. Collingwood\'s theory remained too attached to the romantic model of the artist as emotionally tormented creator; Langer wanted a more impersonal, structurally precise account.' },
        { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Collingwood also resisted treating art as a form of knowledge in Langer\'s sense. For him, art belonged to a domain of pure imaginative experience that was categorically distinct from both science and philosophy. Langer would have found this too concessive — it seemed to agree with the philistine view that art is merely experiential rather than cognitive. The disagreement runs deep: Collingwood\'s idealism located the essence of art in the artist\'s mind, while Langer\'s presentational symbolism located it in an objective formal structure that achieves something no amount of private emotional processing could accomplish.' }
